{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["And the tsadi of הַצְּפִינוֹ has a dagesh like the qof of אִם־יִקְּרֵךְ עָוֺן (1 Samuel 28:10) — to speak of them with precision. And because the tsadi has a sheva na, the pe is weakened though it deserved a dagesh, and it is a verb of the heavy added binyan [nifal]. And there is no need to seek why she did not hide him longer — perhaps the Egyptian neighbors heard his voice, for they were not alone dwelling in the land of Raamses; and it is written: וְשָׁאֲלָה אִשָּׁה מִשְּׁכֶנְתָּהּ (below, 3:22)." "And the phrase \"תיבת גומא\" is mileraʿel because the stress is at the beginning of the word גומא." "<b>And she daubed it.</b> The heh is vowelless like the heh of וַיִּקְרָא לָהּ נֹבַח בְּשְׁמוֹ (Numbers 32:42). And this word is derived from בֶּחֶמֶר, which is red sticky mud, found in the land of Israel; like it: בֶּאֵרֹת בְּאֵרֹת חֶמֶר (Genesis 14:10). And in the Ishmaelite language it is called אל חמר. And Jochebed did this for she said, \"Let me not see the death of the child.\" Perhaps his sister was inspired to do thus. And the thoughts of HaShem are deep, and who can stand in His secret? To Him alone the designs are ascribed. Perhaps HaShem brought this about so that Moses would grow up in the royal house, so that his soul would be on the highest level in learning and habit, and not be low and accustomed to being in a house of slaves. Behold, you see that he killed the Egyptian because he did violence, and he delivered the daughters of Midian from the shepherds because they did violence to water their flocks from the water they drew. And yet another thing: had he grown up among his brothers and they knew him from his youth, they would not fear him, for they would reckon him as one of them." "<b>With pitch.</b> Like קָנֶה וָסוּף קָמְלוּ (Isaiah 19:6); and it is a plant of the river."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The quoted form הַצְּפִינוֹ is not explicitly in the Hebrew source text but is inferred from Ibn Ezra's discussion of the dagesh in the tsadi; I supply it for clarity of the grammatical argument.", 2 "Reading ונחה ה\"א as describing the heh of ותחמרה as vowelless; the comparison is to a quiescent letter that closes a syllable."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:31:47.663031787Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}